<div class="<%= wrapper_class %>">
  <%#
    No tabindex, although daisyUI writes one on its dropdown triggers. It uses
    the attribute to address the trigger in a rule that closes a dropdown again
    while the trigger holds a focus that is not :focus-visible. A tap leaves
    exactly such a focus, and a touch device has no hover to hold the hint open
    instead, so the hint would shut the moment it was asked for. A button is
    focusable without the attribute.
  %>

  <button
    type="button"
    class="<%= trigger_class %>"
    <% if refused %>
      aria-disabled="true"
    <% end %>
  >
    <% if content? %>
      <%= content %>
    <% else %>
      <i class="fa-solid fa-circle-info text-base-content/40" aria-hidden="true"></i>
    <% end %>

    <%#
      The same text a second time. A closed dropdown is display:none and thus
      out of the accessibility tree, so the bubble alone would reach nobody who
      does not see it. The bubble is therefore aria-hidden, and the trigger
      carries the words.
    %>
    <span class="sr-only"><%= text %></span>
  </button>

  <div class="<%= content_class %>" aria-hidden="true"><%= text %></div>
</div>
